1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,080
Previously, on LAiDIES: our heroine
learned that talking to AI isn't coding

2
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:07,520
— it's delegation.

3
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:13,882
She stopped typing three vague words and
hoping, and started briefing the machine

4
00:00:13,882 --> 00:00:19,680
like a smart new hire — David Rose
specific — and got back something she'd
actually send.

5
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:26,169
And on this episode: she gets an answer
with full Regina George confidence,

6
00:00:26,169 --> 00:00:32,380
almost uses it... and then notices one
tiny detail tugging at the corner of the
story.

7
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:36,660
This is Episode Three: The Burn Book
Problem.

8
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:42,620
It's a Thursday afternoon, and the
machine just handed me something that
looks glorious.

9
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:50,700
I'd asked one of the AI tools to turn
four days of messy meeting notes into a
clean client update.

10
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:58,340
Nine seconds later, there it was —
structured, calm, completely sure of
itself.

11
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:03,614
It even opened with "per our
discussion," which I would never, but

12
00:01:03,614 --> 00:01:09,280
somehow coming from the machine it read
as competent instead of
passive-aggressive.

13
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:12,260
I was two clicks from sending.

14
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:15,820
And then one sentence caught my eye.

15
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:20,660
The draft said the client had "approved"
a July rollout.

16
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:24,420
Except — I was in that meeting.

17
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:26,920
Nobody approved anything.

18
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:34,200
What we actually said was "July could
work, if procurement clears by Friday."

19
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:38,200
The machine took a maybe... and gave it
a lanyard.

20
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:44,704
And I couldn't help but wonder... if a
paragraph can sound that sure of itself

21
00:01:44,704 --> 00:01:49,827
— hair done, makeup done, "per our
discussion" and everything — how am I

22
00:01:49,827 --> 00:01:54,740
supposed to catch the one line in it
that's quietly, completely wrong.

23
00:01:56,180 --> 00:02:00,764
Welcome back to LAiDIES — where smart,
busy women learn AI one Wednesday at a

24
00:02:00,764 --> 00:02:03,820
time, from a little internet town called
SUNNYVAiLE.

25
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:10,764
Last week, David Rose taught us to say
what we actually want — fold in the

26
00:02:10,764 --> 00:02:12,680
cheese, but for your inbox.

27
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:19,220
This week, Elle Woods teaches us what to
check before we believe what comes back.

28
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:25,900
Because here's what nobody warns you
about when you start: AI can sound
right.

29
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:30,720
And sounding right is not the same thing
as being right.

30
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:36,020
Before your name goes on it, you need to
know which kind of answer you're
holding.

31
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:42,580
So I did what this town has taught me —
I shut the laptop and took it to Main
Street.

32
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:48,315
To the NewsStand on Main, whose job is
separating what actually happened from

33
00:02:48,315 --> 00:02:50,180
what just sounds like it did.

34
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:57,883
No doom, no hype, no "a source close to
the situation." And standing there with

35
00:02:57,883 --> 00:03:03,700
the real headlines, it landed: my draft
didn't need a better writer.

36
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:09,669
It needed someone at the news desk,
holding every confident line up to the

37
00:03:09,669 --> 00:03:11,900
light — says who? based on what?

38
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:15,180
The machine had handed me a front page.

39
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:20,060
My job was to fact-check it before it
ran under my byline.

40
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:23,440
Let me tell you about the Burn Book.

41
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:29,140
It didn't work because it was true —
none of it was, and nobody checked.

42
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:35,120
It worked because it had social
authority: a rumor, a grudge, a wild

43
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:40,895
guess, and something fully unhinged, all
in the same handwriting, with the same

44
00:03:40,895 --> 00:03:43,060
devastating teenage certainty.

45
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:50,391
AI writes in that same hand — that same
certainty — but here's the twist: it

46
00:03:50,391 --> 00:03:52,400
doesn't only write rumors.

47
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:58,646
It'll take a real source, an old source,
a similar-but-not-this source, and an

48
00:03:58,646 --> 00:04:04,160
assumption it made because the pattern
looked familiar, and hand you one smooth

49
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:07,400
paragraph like everyone in it belongs
together.

50
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:13,788
That's the Burn Book Problem: an
official court record and an entry in

51
00:04:13,788 --> 00:04:19,202
the Burn Book come out in that exact
same handwriting — same confidence, same

52
00:04:19,202 --> 00:04:23,020
finish — you can't tell which is which
until you check.

53
00:04:23,780 --> 00:04:29,900
Take the Bethany Byrd moment from Mean
Girls — a perfect, tiny sourcing
disaster.

54
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:36,426
Somebody ( Regina) writes in the Burn
Book that Bethany must be lying about

55
00:04:36,426 --> 00:04:40,380
being a virgin — because she buys
super-jumbo tampons.

56
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:45,720
One box of tampons, and boom — a
verdict, filed.

57
00:04:46,540 --> 00:04:52,342
Then the actual explanation walks in,
much less scandalous and much more

58
00:04:52,342 --> 00:04:58,860
specific: she's just got a heavy flow
and a wide set.....well you know the
rest...

59
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:06,480
That was never evidence. It's a clue in
a Claire's headband, sprinting straight
to a conclusion.

60
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:11,380
One data point. No context. Enormous
conclusion.

61
00:05:12,140 --> 00:05:17,760
So the question is never "can I use AI?"
Yes. Use it.

62
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:20,620
We are not here to churn butter by
candlelight.

63
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:26,738
The question is: which parts is it just
drafting for you — and which parts are

64
00:05:26,738 --> 00:05:30,260
claims that need receipts before they
borrow your name?

65
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:36,443
Now, a few wrong answers are easy to
spot — the product that doesn't exist,

66
00:05:36,443 --> 00:05:38,820
the answer that argues with itself.

67
00:05:39,690 --> 00:05:41,100
But the fake citation?

68
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:43,420
That one's dressed to pass.

69
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:49,190
It looks exactly like a real source —
cited with the confidence of "my

70
00:05:49,190 --> 00:05:55,000
boyfriend goes to another school" —
right up until you click it and it goes
nowhere.

71
00:05:56,080 --> 00:05:58,840
And the sneakiest one isn't fake at all.

72
00:05:59,580 --> 00:06:02,080
It's real information in the wrong
place.

73
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:08,570
It brought the wrong ID and somehow made
it past the door. A U.S.

74
00:06:09,430 --> 00:06:11,650
HR answer in a Canadian workplace.

75
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:16,050
Last year's pricing page wearing this
year's lip gloss.

76
00:06:17,390 --> 00:06:23,487
"We talked about it" quietly promoted to
"we decided." A policy answer that's

77
00:06:23,487 --> 00:06:28,490
technically true, except the exception
is the part that matters.

78
00:06:29,810 --> 00:06:34,911
That's the moment you stand up in the
back, in your blue hoodie, strings

79
00:06:34,911 --> 00:06:40,990
pulled tight, and your oversized
sunglasses, and you shout: "SHE DOESN'T
EVEN GO HERE!"

80
00:06:41,850 --> 00:06:43,330
It's not just a great line.

81
00:06:43,870 --> 00:06:45,750
It's a quality-control standard.

82
00:06:46,670 --> 00:06:50,870
And this is where Elle Woods becomes the
patron saint of AI verification.

83
00:06:51,830 --> 00:06:56,951
Not because she makes "being thorough"
sound corporate — because she spots the

84
00:06:56,951 --> 00:07:02,590
one detail everyone else treated like
lip gloss and realizes it's holding up
the alibi.

85
00:07:03,750 --> 00:07:09,290
Chutney says she was in the shower,
right after getting a perm. Elle asks
again.

86
00:07:10,270 --> 00:07:12,050
Chutney gives the same story.

87
00:07:12,810 --> 00:07:17,005
The room rolls its eyes, because it
sounds like Elle is making haircare

88
00:07:17,005 --> 00:07:19,510
small talk in the middle of a murder
trial.

89
00:07:20,490 --> 00:07:23,330
But Elle isn't checking whether Chutney
can repeat herself.

90
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:26,450
She's waiting for the detail that
doesn't fit.

91
00:07:27,270 --> 00:07:32,754
And if you know one thing about perms,
you know you do not wash a fresh one —

92
00:07:32,754 --> 00:07:38,590
not unless you're trying to destroy your
alibi and your hair in the same
afternoon.

93
00:07:40,050 --> 00:07:45,670
One tiny beauty-world rule nobody took
seriously... and the story falls apart.

94
00:07:46,790 --> 00:07:48,870
With AI, that's the job.

95
00:07:49,790 --> 00:07:53,850
Don't ask "does this sound smart?"
Chutney sounded smart.

96
00:07:54,370 --> 00:07:56,070
She sounded smart three times.

97
00:07:57,190 --> 00:08:01,590
Ask: what's the one detail that can't
survive contact with the timeline?

98
00:08:02,530 --> 00:08:06,190
If it says a policy changed — what date
proves it?

99
00:08:06,870 --> 00:08:10,510
If it says a quote came from a person —
where's the quote?

100
00:08:11,550 --> 00:08:17,570
If it says a number went up — from what,
over what timeframe, according to whom?

101
00:08:18,670 --> 00:08:20,510
Do not be Chutney on the stand.

102
00:08:21,350 --> 00:08:22,890
Be Elle with the timeline.

103
00:08:23,910 --> 00:08:28,050
So before an answer leaves your laptop,
sort it into three piles.

104
00:08:28,890 --> 00:08:34,590
A draft is wording, structure, a
brainstorm, a summary — that can be
fast.

105
00:08:35,610 --> 00:08:41,651
A claim is a name, a date, a number, a
quote, a source, anything legal, HR,

106
00:08:41,651 --> 00:08:44,830
privacy, or money — that needs checking.

107
00:08:45,650 --> 00:08:52,110
And a receipt is the thing you can
actually open, name, or point to if
someone asks.

108
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:54,910
A draft is an outfit.

109
00:08:55,550 --> 00:08:58,850
A claim is an alibi. Dress accordingly.

110
00:09:00,310 --> 00:09:06,470
And before this sounds like a second job
— remember who you are here. You're
Elle.

111
00:09:06,830 --> 00:09:11,740
You're the one who stands up in that
courtroom, finds the detail that doesn't

112
00:09:11,740 --> 00:09:14,510
hold up, and wins with her name on the
case.

113
00:09:15,550 --> 00:09:16,950
Nobody hands you that part.

114
00:09:18,310 --> 00:09:23,310
But here's what Elle didn't have: a law
clerk who already did the reading.

115
00:09:24,510 --> 00:09:28,886
The machine spent the weekend in the
library so you didn't — it did the

116
00:09:28,886 --> 00:09:30,770
research, it drafted the brief.

117
00:09:31,670 --> 00:09:37,943
You still read it, you still check the
citations, you still walk in and own it

118
00:09:37,943 --> 00:09:42,390
— because when the judge looks up, she's
looking at you.

119
00:09:43,470 --> 00:09:45,230
That's not extra work.

120
00:09:46,030 --> 00:09:50,370
The grunt work got done for you; the
judgment stayed yours.

121
00:09:51,650 --> 00:09:57,706
And please — asking AI "are you sure?"
is like asking Regina George whether the

122
00:09:57,706 --> 00:10:03,270
Burn Book is peer reviewed. Bold choice.
Limited value.

123
00:10:04,870 --> 00:10:08,839
Sometimes it catches the mistake,
corrects itself, and we all briefly

124
00:10:08,839 --> 00:10:10,370
believe in personal growth.

125
00:10:11,110 --> 00:10:16,738
And sometimes it just hands you the same
wrong answer in the same questionable

126
00:10:16,738 --> 00:10:22,295
outfit consisting of two popped-collar
polos, one over the other (of course),

127
00:10:22,295 --> 00:10:27,430
but this time with the shirt order
switched. That's not verification.

128
00:10:28,310 --> 00:10:32,697
That's Chutney repeating the alibi in
the same questionable outfit, just with

129
00:10:32,697 --> 00:10:34,610
a slightly different colour combo.

130
00:10:35,350 --> 00:10:40,380
Now — before someone in the back adjusts
a butterfly clip and says, "but the

131
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:43,670
tools are getting better" — yes. They
are.

132
00:10:44,490 --> 00:10:49,382
The newer ones can search, cite, read
documents you hand them, and sometimes

133
00:10:49,382 --> 00:10:50,970
flag when they're unsure.

134
00:10:51,830 --> 00:10:53,690
That's real, and it helps.

135
00:10:54,850 --> 00:10:56,690
But better is not solved.

136
00:10:57,410 --> 00:11:03,383
A 2026 Nature paper put a name to why:
the way we grade these models rewards a

137
00:11:03,383 --> 00:11:08,070
confident guess over an honest "I don't
know" — so they guess.

138
00:11:09,110 --> 00:11:15,003
Stanford's 2026 AI Index found something
scarier for our purposes: tell a top

139
00:11:15,003 --> 00:11:21,350
model something false that you seem to
believe, and it'll often just agree with
you.

140
00:11:22,030 --> 00:11:26,902
And KPMG — one of the Big Four
accounting firms, the serious-suit

141
00:11:26,902 --> 00:11:32,659
people — had to pull an AI report after
someone checked its sources and found

142
00:11:32,659 --> 00:11:37,250
forty of the forty-five were made up.
Forty of forty-five.

143
00:11:38,850 --> 00:11:41,890
Big Four. Tiny receipt drawer.

144
00:11:43,010 --> 00:11:45,510
So attach the sources, absolutely.

145
00:11:46,350 --> 00:11:51,876
Just don't assume "sources attached"
means "sources checked." Sometimes it's

146
00:11:51,876 --> 00:11:56,110
still Chutney, handing you a folder in
her own handwriting.

147
00:11:57,010 --> 00:12:02,152
And the good news is, the serious
guidance — from OpenAI, Anthropic,

148
00:12:02,152 --> 00:12:07,890
Google, Stanford — all points the same
way, and it comes down to three moves.

149
00:12:09,290 --> 00:12:11,350
Move one: give her the source.

150
00:12:12,310 --> 00:12:17,895
Don't ask the machine what it remembers
— paste the real document, the actual

151
00:12:17,895 --> 00:12:24,410
policy, this year's pricing page; when
you need something current, turn on its
search mode.

152
00:12:25,210 --> 00:12:32,630
Then one line: "answer only from what I
just gave you." Elle doesn't argue from
memory.

153
00:12:33,230 --> 00:12:34,930
She walks in with the file.

154
00:12:36,150 --> 00:12:42,917
Move two: let her say "I don't know."
Add the sentence most people never think

155
00:12:42,917 --> 00:12:49,599
to add — "if it's not in there, say so;
don't fill the gap." That one line is

156
00:12:49,599 --> 00:12:54,310
what turns a confident guess back into
an honest blank.

157
00:12:55,810 --> 00:12:59,310
Move three: make her show the line by
asking:

158
00:13:00,290 --> 00:13:02,850
"Quote the exact sentence you got that
from."

159
00:13:03,610 --> 00:13:08,200
If she can't point to it, that claim
showed up at Spring Fling with no

160
00:13:08,200 --> 00:13:10,850
student ID — and it doesn't get to
speak.

161
00:13:12,170 --> 00:13:16,190
One rule over all three: no invented
receipts.

162
00:13:17,030 --> 00:13:20,110
No made-up links, dates, quotes, or
numbers.

163
00:13:21,170 --> 00:13:23,490
Watch it work on my Thursday disaster.

164
00:13:24,610 --> 00:13:29,201
Instead of "clean up these notes," I
pasted the notes and said: summarize

165
00:13:29,201 --> 00:13:33,916
only what's here, mark anything we
didn't actually decide as "pending," and

166
00:13:33,916 --> 00:13:36,150
show me the line behind every claim.

167
00:13:37,370 --> 00:13:43,810
Same nine seconds — no phantom July
approval, because I never left a gap for
the machine to fill.

168
00:13:45,070 --> 00:13:51,790
That's Prompt Like Elle: hand her the
file, let her say "I don't know," make
her show the line.

169
00:13:52,910 --> 00:13:57,790
Which brings me to my favourite part of
every episode — the cocktail party
explanation.

170
00:13:58,710 --> 00:14:03,977
The answer you'll give the next time
someone at happy hour puts down her

171
00:14:03,977 --> 00:14:10,110
drink and asks, "wait — why does it just
make things up?" Here it is: it's not
lying.

172
00:14:11,150 --> 00:14:13,470
Lying takes intent. It's guessing.

173
00:14:14,470 --> 00:14:18,839
When the machine doesn't know, it
doesn't stop — it reaches for the most

174
00:14:18,839 --> 00:14:22,310
plausible-sounding thing and says it
with its whole chest.

175
00:14:23,530 --> 00:14:25,010
It's your most confident friend.

176
00:14:25,650 --> 00:14:29,730
The one who will answer any question,
whether or not she actually knows.

177
00:14:30,670 --> 00:14:33,090
The fix was never to catch her in a lie.

178
00:14:33,770 --> 00:14:37,630
It's to ask for the receipt before you
repeat her in a meeting.

179
00:14:39,290 --> 00:14:41,210
And... that's the episode.

180
00:14:41,990 --> 00:14:43,650
Now — your try-on.

181
00:14:44,450 --> 00:14:49,090
Ten minutes, not homework, and
